Exception:
    -   An exception is an unwanted and unexpected event that terminates the normal flow of
        program.
    -   Example:
        -   Division by zero
        -   Accessing invalid index
        -   Using null reference

                                    Throwable
                                    ____|____
                                 Error      Exception
                                            ____|_______
                                            |           |
                                         Checked       Unchecked
                                            |               |
                                    Compile-time checked  Run-time Checked
                                            |                  |
                              IOException, SQLException     ArithmeticException,
                                                            NullPointerException,
                                                            ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.

Error:
    -   An error is a serious problem that occurs during program execution and cannot be
        handled by the program.
    -   Example:
        -   StackOverFlowError
        -   OutOfMemoryError
        -   VirtualMachineError

Blocks in Exception Handling:
    -   try
        -   try block contains doubtful statements that may cause of exception
        -   try block must be followed by catch or finally block
    -   catch
        -   catch block used to handle the exception thrown by try block
        -   we can handle multiple exceptions in catch block
    -   finally
        -   finally block always executed, no matter exception occurred or not.
        -   finally block used to execute cleanup code.
        -   finally block is skipped only if System.exit() is called or JVM crashes.

    NOTE:
        -   we can write try with finally, without catch block.
        -   but we can not write catch without try block

throw and throws
    -   throw
        -   throw is used inside method or block of code
        -   throw is used to explicitly throw an exception
        -   throw throws one exception object at a time
        -   throw followed by an exception object
        e.g. -> throw new ArithmeticException();

    -   throws
        -   throws is used in method declaration
        -   declares exceptions that a method may propagates to its caller
        -   throws can declare multiple exception types, separated by commas
        -   throws followed by exception class names.
        e.g -> throws IOException, SQLException

    IMP POINTS
        -   throw works with both checked and unchecked exceptions
        -   throws is especially important for declaring checked exceptions, but it can
            also declare unchecked exceptions

Custom Exception
    -   Why do we need Custom Exception
        -   Application specific error handling
        -   Code is readable and meaningful
        -   meaningful message for user
        -   best practice for real time projects.

    - How to create custom Exceptions
        -   Two ways of creating custom exception
            1.  extending Exception class
                =   in this case (Checked Exceptions),
                    compiler will force to handle this exception
            2.  extending RuntimeException class
                =   (Unchecked Exceptions), compiler does not force to handle them

    NOTE: Checked exception is for Business rule violation and
          Unchecked for programing or validation error


Multiple Catch Block in Java
    -   Multiple catch block is used when one try catch have multiple exceptions.
    POINTS TO REMEMBER
    -   if try block have multiple exceptions, try always handles first exception
    -   catch block execute in top to bottom exception propagation
    -   specific exception is always write before the Parent Exception